# LeakSanitizer suppressions for G-LSAN-GTK (.port/tools/gates/sanitizers.sh
# --gate lsan-gtk), read AFTER lsan.supp (the lane concatenates the two).
#
# The lane runs one live GTK test (gtk_window_live: a real Window with a core
# Surface, created and closed) under ASan with detect_leaks=1 inside a headless
# sway. Same rules as lsan.supp: every entry is THIRD-PARTY process-lifetime
# state (the GTK/GLib/fontconfig stack below a call we make), each with a row in
# .port/phase-2/SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md (G ids) giving the reason, the
# evidence and the negative control that keeps a Rust leak RED, and a WHY
# comment directly above it (D6 rule c). A leak in Rustty code is never
# suppressed: it is a finding. LSan treats a suppressed block as live, so an
# entry also hides what that block points to: each entry has a ceiling on its
# match count (sanitizers.sh LSAN_GTK_CEILINGS; RED above it).
#
# G2 (gtk4-rs's BuilderRustScope template-callback closures, NEW-FINDINGS N516)
# was removed in P7-W25: the port binds template callbacks through GTK's C
# builder scope, as the reference does, and nothing leaks there.
#
# Format: one `leak:<pattern>` per line, as lsan.supp.

# G1 (SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md): the system Pango's fontconfig font-set
# results. libpangoft2 enumerates and matches fonts (FcFontSetList,
# FcFontSetMatch) for GTK's widgets, and the FcPatterns it gets back are
# unreachable at exit; every report has libpangoft2 below the fontconfig
# allocation and no Rustty frame. A pattern Rustty's own font code leaks has no
# libpangoft2 frame (G-SAN-SELF A2g "leaked FcPattern" stays RED).
leak:libpangoft2-1.0.so
# G1b (SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md, with G1): the same Pango font map's
# patterns through its cairo layer (libpangocairo calls FcConfigSubstitute on
# a pattern it keeps); no Rustty frame. Same negative control as G1.
leak:libpangocairo-1.0.so
